Psoriatic Arthritis
Rheumatology & Immunology
DIP joint involvement, Dactylitis, Nail pitting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-036
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of unilateral dactylitis and painful distal joint swelling, rendering the patient unable to utilize the hand for basic functions and causing severe sleep disruption.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, intensely painful, "sausage-like" swelling of a single digit with concurrent severe mechanical joint pain. An urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively exclude an acute flexor tenosynovitis or septic arthritis (surgical emergencies) from a severe dactylitic flare, and to provide immediate, safe bridging analgesia, as delaying until standard hours posed a risk of permanent tendon damage or unmanageable distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My left index finger has swollen up like a hot sausage, the joints at the very tips of my fingers are throbbing, and my nails are crumbling."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-day history of an acute flare characterized by asymmetric oligoarthritis, prominently involving the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints of the left hand. Over the past 24 hours, the left index finger has developed severe dactylitis, manifesting as a uniformly swollen, painful 'sausage digit'. Concurrently, the patient notes exquisite tenderness at the left Achilles tendon insertion (enthesitis), causing a pronounced limp. Reports severe morning stiffness exceeding 60 minutes. The patient has known, well-demarcated erythematous skin plaques with silvery scale on their elbows and knees. Denies current eye pain (uveitis) or inflammatory back pain.
Functional Status: Housebound, completely unable to perform fine motor tasks with the left hand; ambulating with a severe limp.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; functional impairment of the dominant hand severely limits self-care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears fatigued and distressed, guarding the left hand closely.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: The left index finger exhibits profound dactylitis—it is uniformly swollen, erythematous, and tender along the entire length, restricting both active and passive flexion. The DIP joints of the left 2nd and 3rd digits are swollen and tender. The Achilles tendon on the left is thickened and exquisitely tender to palpation at the calcaneal insertion. Dermatological: Classic psoriatic plaques present on extensor surfaces (elbows). Nails demonstrate severe pitting, onycholysis, and subungual hyperkeratosis. Diagnostic tetrad heavily supported: prominent DIP joint involvement, striking clinical dactylitis, and classic nail pitting, strongly predicting future 'pencil-in-cup' deformity on hand radiography with negative rheumatoid factor and negative anti-CCP antibodies.
Environmental Observations: Pre-prepared meals present; patient advised on one-handed opening techniques.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter paracetamol with zero relief of the dactylitic pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Psoriatic Arthritis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute dactylitis/enthesitis flare)
Differentials:

Infectious Flexor Tenosynovitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a penetrating trauma history, the presence of concurrent DIP arthritis and Achilles enthesitis pointing to a systemic flare, and the lack of Kanavel's cardinal signs (specifically, severe pain on passive extension is less prominent than the global digit tenderness of dactylitis).

Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic involvement of the DIP joints (which RA spares), the presence of profound dactylitis, and the striking psoriatic skin and nail changes.

Acute Gout - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dactylitic (whole digit) pattern rather than a single joint (like the first MTP), the presence of classic psoriatic stigmata, and the more subacute (3-day) evolution.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an acute, severe flare of Psoriatic Arthritis, highlighted by excruciating dactylitis and Achilles enthesitis. Differentiating a dactylitic digit from an infectious flexor tenosynovitis is a critical clinical step that cannot be performed safely over the phone. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was mandated to rule out this surgical emergency, assess the functional loss of the hand, and initiate high-dose targeted systemic anti-inflammatories. Leaving this patient unmedicated until the next standard consultation would have resulted in agonizing pain and prolonged functional impairment, strictly justifying the MBS 585-600 service provision.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate a scheduled, high-dose NSAID regimen (e.g., Naproxen 500mg BID or Indomethacin) combined with a proton pump inhibitor, pending normal renal function, to aggressively treat the enthesitis and dactylitis. Alternatively, a short tapering course of oral corticosteroids (Prednisolone) if NSAIDs are contraindicated. Arrange an urgent referral to Rheumatology for consideration of DMARD or biologic therapy escalation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Localized ice therapy: Apply a small, wrapped ice pack directly to the swollen digit and the Achilles tendon for 10 minutes every two hours; demonstrating that this rapidly numbs the superficial pain receptors and visibly reduces the vascular throbbing directly reinforces adherence to conservative localized management. Functional resting: Utilize a simple finger splint or tape the index and middle fingers together (buddy taping); executing this mechanical stabilization immediately reduces the agonizing pain of accidental flexion, proving the value of joint protection during the acute phase.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Buddy tape applied.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48-72 hours to assess response to acute anti-inflammatory therapy and arrange specialist referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the swollen finger becomes intensely hot, develops a spreading red streak up the arm, if you spike a high fever (>38.5 C), or if you develop a sudden, severe, painful red eye (uveitis).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe swelling of the entire finger and the tendon pain are classic severe flares of your underlying psoriasis affecting the joints. We have ruled out a dangerous bacterial tendon infection today.
Clinical Course: The acute dactylitis will usually begin to recede within 3-5 days of high-dose anti-inflammatory treatment. Long-term, you require specialized immune-modulating medications to prevent permanent joint destruction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to force the swollen finger to bend. If you develop a high fever, uncontrollable shivering, or if the redness begins rapidly tracking up your hand and forearm, you must go to the emergency department immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is aggressively attacking the entire tendon sheath of your finger and the insertion point of your heel cord, causing them to engorge with fluid. The high-dose anti-inflammatories are required to chemically force this swelling down, as standard painkillers cannot stop the active immune attack.
Actionable Steps: Take the NSAIDs strictly with meals. Keep the finger taped to its neighbor to prevent painful movement. Apply ice locally to the heel and finger to reduce the throbbing.

